WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:08.757
Minimal APIs often rely on broader ASP.NET
Core middleware and hosting controls.

2
00:00:08.837 --> 00:00:14.582
The endpoint handler is only one part of
the request path.

3
00:00:14.662 --> 00:00:22.680
CORS, CSRF prevention, rate limiting,
security headers, request body limits,

4
00:00:22.760 --> 00:00:28.145
upload controls, decompression behavior,
logging,

5
00:00:28.225 --> 00:00:35.884
and hosting-layer limits may all influence
whether the endpoint is safe and

6
00:00:35.964 --> 00:00:37.041
operable.

7
00:00:37.121 --> 00:00:45.738
CORS controls browser cross-origin read
access and does not replace authentication

8
00:00:45.818 --> 00:00:47.733
or authorization.

9
00:00:47.813 --> 00:00:55.831
A Minimal API endpoint that allows a
browser origin still needs a clear identity

10
00:00:55.911 --> 00:00:58.424
model and access policy.

11
00:00:58.504 --> 00:01:03.291
CORS should describe approved browser
access,

12
00:01:03.371 --> 00:01:07.799
not act as the API's main security
boundary.

13
00:01:07.879 --> 00:01:16.854
Cookie-authenticated browser APIs may need
CSRF prevention for unsafe state-changing

14
00:01:16.934 --> 00:01:18.011
requests.

15
00:01:18.091 --> 00:01:23.716
A browser can attach cookies according to
cookie rules,

16
00:01:23.796 --> 00:01:31.335
so an API that uses cookies should have a
deliberate request-intent model.

17
00:01:31.415 --> 00:01:37.997
Bearer-token APIs have different browser
credential behavior,

18
00:01:38.077 --> 00:01:44.420
but they still need careful token handling
and authorization.

19
00:01:44.500 --> 00:01:52.638
Rate limiting and request controls help
protect availability, fairness, cost,

20
00:01:52.718 --> 00:01:55.111
and backend resources.

21
00:01:55.191 --> 00:02:02.850
Minimal API endpoints that perform
searches, uploads, report generation,

22
00:02:02.930 --> 00:02:11.427
state changes, or tenant-wide operations
may need different limits from cheap read

23
00:02:11.507 --> 00:02:12.704
endpoints.

24
00:02:12.784 --> 00:02:21.281
Middleware order and endpoint scope
determine whether policies apply as intended.

25
00:02:21.361 --> 00:02:27.105
Security headers matter for browser-facing
responses.

26
00:02:27.185 --> 00:02:35.203
HSTS, Content Security Policy, framing
controls, nosniff, Referrer-Policy,

27
00:02:35.283 --> 00:02:44.019
and Permissions-Policy can help constrain
browser behavior when applied carefully.

28
00:02:44.099 --> 00:02:52.476
They do not replace secure API logic, but
they support a safer browser environment.

29
00:02:52.556 --> 00:02:56.506
Request body limits, upload controls,

30
00:02:56.586 --> 00:03:03.048
and decompression behavior should be
reviewed where relevant.

31
00:03:03.128 --> 00:03:09.590
A Minimal API endpoint that accepts large
bodies, files, forms,

32
00:03:09.670 --> 00:03:16.851
or compressed input should have explicit
limits and failure behavior.

33
00:03:16.931 --> 00:03:25.188
Cross-cutting controls are strongest when
they are scoped to real endpoint needs

34
00:03:25.268 --> 00:03:28.260
rather than applied blindly.
